Context

Deutsche Bank has invested in powerful GPU infrastructure, with dbLLM already deploying open-weight AI models and Ada providing an ecosystem for AI asset discovery.

Meanwhile, developers increasingly use external AI intelligence through tools such as GitHub Copilot and Gemini CLI. Modern coding agents are capable of going beyond code suggestions to investigate complex bugs, modify code, execute tests, and complete multi-step engineering workflows.

These advances present an opportunity to explore whether Deutsche Bank can leverage its existing infrastructure to build and operate similarly capable AI agents internally.

Problem Statement

Can Deutsche Bank use its existing GPU infrastructure and open-weight models to deliver powerful, secure, and economically competitive AI agents without depending exclusively on external AI providers?

While models can already be hosted internally, their ability to power practical, end-to-end coding agents through an extensible harness such as Pi or OpenCode has not yet been validated.

There are three key opportunities:

* Cost: External AI services can introduce recurring usage and subscription costs. We need to determine whether internally hosted models can deliver competitive quality and latency at a lower long-term cost.
* Security and data control: External AI services may require sensitive code, prompts, or documents to be processed outside bank-controlled inference infrastructure. Self-hosted models offer the opportunity to keep this processing within approved internal boundaries, subject to appropriate security controls.
* Internal capability: Without reusable agent and model integrations, the bank risks limiting the practical use of its GPU investments and continuing to depend on external providers for capabilities that may be developed and maintained internally.

Business Impact and Validation

We propose to validate this opportunity through a concrete coding-agent experiment, comparing a Pi/OpenCode harness powered by an internally hosted open-weight model against existing Gemini CLI and Copilot-assisted workflows.

We will measure task accuracy, latency, developer intervention, and fully loaded cost per successful task. A hypothetical Claude Code subscription scenario will provide an additional commercial cost reference.

Successful validation could establish the foundation for broader adoption of internally hosted AI, combining dbLLM’s model-serving capabilities with Ada’s existing ecosystem to make open-weight models a practical choice across engineering and other enterprise workflows.